<article class="col lead">
  <h1>My Doctor Took Away My Pills. What My Friend Told Me Next Is Something <em class="hl">Most People Get Completely Wrong</em>.</h1>
  <p class="dek">Two weeks later I almost threw the jar in the trash. I’m glad I read one more page first.</p>
</article>

<figure class="hero col">
  <div class="hero__frame">
    <img src="{{ 'dhd-r23-hero.webp' | asset_url }}" alt="Jack, a contractor in a red flannel shirt, at his kitchen table beside his pills, coffee and a bloodwork printout" width="1600" height="1195" fetchpriority="high">
  </div>
  <div class="byline" data-copy-status="scripted-editorial-persona">
    <img class="byline__photo" src="{{ 'dhd-r23-jack.webp' | asset_url }}" alt="Jack R." width="240" height="240">
    <div class="byline__text"><b>By Jack R.</b><span>Dayton, Ohio · <time datetime="2026-09-12">Sep 12, 2026</time> · 3 min read</span></div>
  </div>
</figure>

<article class="col body story">

<p class="lead-p">At 6:42 every morning, I lined up my pills beside my coffee before I put on my boots.</p>
<p>Ibuprofen for my back. Acetaminophen for my knee. And a prescription acid reducer.</p>
<p>After four years of that routine, my stomach had started fighting back.</p>
<p>I’m 54. I’ve poured concrete and framed houses in Ohio for thirty years.</p>
<p>In my trade, you don’t talk about your back. You get up, put your boots on, and do the work.</p>
<p>So I hid it. Some mornings I sat in my truck at the job site, not checking the plans.</p>
<p>I was waiting for my back to settle enough to climb out.</p>
<p>I carried one tool at a time. I used the truck door to pull myself up.</p>
<p>If one of the guys noticed, I blamed the cold.</p>
<p>My wife noticed anyway.</p>
<p>One morning she found me sitting on the bottom stair with one boot on.</p>
<p>When she asked if I needed help, I told her I was tying my laces.</p>
<p>I wasn’t.</p>

<h2 class="story-turn" data-reveal>The Appointment I Kept Putting Off</h2>

<p>She finally made me get bloodwork.</p>
<p>I’d put it off for a year, because I already had a feeling what the doctor would say.</p>


<p>A week later he turned his screen toward me.</p>
<p>He pointed at two lines and set my ibuprofen bottle on the counter between us.</p>

<blockquote class="quote" data-reveal><p>“Your kidney numbers are moving the wrong way. And your stomach is taking a beating.</p><p>You need to stop taking ibuprofen every day.”</p></blockquote>

<figure class="fig" data-reveal><div class="fig__frame"><img src="{{ 'dhd-r28-lab-arm.webp' | asset_url }}" alt="Sunburned forearm on a blue lab chair armrest, a cotton ball taped inside the elbow, the open hand grey with concrete dust" width="1600" height="1195" loading="lazy"></div></figure>

<p>I looked down at my hands. I knew he was right.</p>
<p>I also had a concrete delivery at <strong>7&nbsp;AM</strong> the next morning.</p>
<p>I had a crew to pay and a back that hurt before I even stood up.</p>
<p>“So what am I supposed to take at 6&nbsp;AM?” I asked him.</p>
<p>Ten minutes later I was sitting in my truck in the parking lot.</p>
<p>A prescription for <strong>Tramadol</strong> was on the passenger seat.</p>

<h2 class="story-turn" data-reveal>Nothing Prepared Me to Be Afraid of a Piece of Paper</h2>

<p>My older brother spent six years on prescription painkillers after his shoulder surgery.</p>
<p>I remember the foggy phone calls, and him losing his place halfway through a sentence.</p>
<p>I remember how hard it was for him to come off them.</p>
<p>That stayed with me a lot longer than his surgery did.</p>
<p>Now his whole story was sitting on my passenger seat.</p>
<p>I kept looking from that paper to the job site through the windshield.</p>

<figure class="fig" data-reveal><div class="fig__frame"><img src="{{ 'dhd-r26-prescription.webp' | asset_url }}" alt="A folded prescription on a pickup truck’s passenger seat beside work gloves, a parking lot through the windshield" width="1600" height="1195" loading="lazy"></div></figure>

<p>Keep taking the pills my doctor had just told me to stop. Or start the one I was scared of.</p>

<aside class="pull" data-reveal><p>Neither one felt like a choice I wanted to make.</p></aside>

<p>Tomorrow was already set: the delivery, the crew, the forms to sign.</p>
<p>My body was the only part of the schedule I couldn’t count on.</p>

<h2 class="story-turn" data-reveal>The Talk in Dale’s Shop</h2>

<p>That evening I drove over to Dale’s shop.</p>
<p>We’ve worked side by side for twenty years.</p>
<p>He’s seen me pour footings in January and sit in my truck longer than I needed to.</p>
<p>I told him about the bloodwork and the paper in my truck.</p>
<p>“I’m supposed to stop the ibuprofen,” I said. “I still have to work tomorrow.”</p>
<p>He was quiet for a second.</p>
<p>Then he said, “Jack, I was sitting exactly where you are three years ago.”</p>

<figure class="fig fig--portrait" data-reveal><div class="fig__frame"><img src="{{ 'dhd-r26-dale.webp' | asset_url }}" alt="Dale, a grey haired carpenter, at his workbench, smiling at the camera" width="1280" height="1600" loading="lazy"></div></figure>

<p>I didn’t know that.</p>
<p>He’s been kneeling on subfloors and carrying lumber up stairs since he was nineteen.</p>
<p>I always figured he was just tougher than me.</p>
<p>“I was eating ibuprofen like candy,” he said.</p>
<p>“Then my doctor told me the same thing yours did.”</p>
<p>“I spent a whole winter gritting my teeth through jobs, dreading the drive home.”</p>
<p>What changed, he said, was a jar his nephew put in his hand.</p>
<p>A capsule made from the leaf of a tree called <i>Mitragyna speciosa</i>. Its main compound is called&nbsp;<strong>mitragynine</strong>.</p>
<p>“I didn’t believe it either,” he said.</p>
<p>“But I’ve taken it most mornings since. It’s the thing I rely on now.”</p>
<p>He told me farmers and laborers in Southeast Asia have used the leaves for centuries.</p>
<p>People with jobs as hard on their bodies as ours.</p>
<p>Before I left, he texted me a link to the capsules he uses.</p>

<figure class="sms" data-reveal aria-label="Text message from Dale">
  <p class="sms__time">Tuesday 7:48 PM</p>
  <p class="sms__from">Dale</p>
  <p class="sms__bubble">This is the capsule I told you about. Read this first.</p>
  <div class="sms__card"><img src="{{ 'dhd-r25-leaf.webp' | asset_url }}" alt="" width="1536" height="1152" loading="lazy"><p><i>Mitragyna speciosa</i> Leaf Extract Capsules</p></div>
</figure>

<p>I looked at the page, closed it, opened it again, and ordered a jar.</p>

<h2 class="story-turn" data-reveal>I Stood Up Before I Thought About My Back</h2>

<p>The jar came midweek.</p>
<p>I waited until Saturday to try one at home, with nowhere to be.</p>
<p>After a little while, I felt a clean lift, with warmth spreading through my arms and legs.</p>
<p>It was deeply relaxing, and somehow put me in a better mood.</p>
<p>Then the doorbell rang.</p>
<p>I got up to answer it and realized I <strong>hadn’t planted both hands on my knees first</strong>.</p>
<p>The ache in my back had eased enough that I just moved.</p>
<p>On the way back I bent down and picked up the shoes I’d left in the hallway.</p>
<p>They were the same shoes I’d been stepping around all week.</p>
<p>After breakfast I carried my coffee upstairs.</p>
<p>Halfway up, I noticed my free hand was hanging at my side. It wasn’t pulling me along the rail.</p>
<p>I stopped on the landing and looked back at the stairs.</p>
<p>I had just walked up my own stairs without thinking about every step.</p>
<p>That afternoon I finally watered the tomato plants I’d been meaning to get to all week.</p>
<p>Then I sat on the porch with nothing left on my list. My wife took this picture.</p>

<figure class="fig" data-reveal><div class="fig__frame"><img src="{{ 'dhd-r26-porch.webp' | asset_url }}" alt="Jack on his back porch step in late afternoon, boots off, coffee in hand, smiling at the camera, tomato plants behind him" width="1600" height="1195" loading="lazy"></div></figure>

<p>I’d been saving every bit of strength for paid work.</p>
<p>Having some left over for a regular Saturday felt good.</p>
<p>That night, before bed, I looked up mitragynine.</p>

</article>


<section class="mechanism" aria-labelledby="mechanism-title">
