<h2 id="mechanism-title" data-reveal>What I Learned About Mitragynine</h2>

<p>Mitragynine interacts with receptors involved in pain signaling.</p>
<p>In laboratory experiments, it produced a smaller response than compounds that fully activate those receptors.</p>
<p>Researchers call this <strong>partial activation</strong>.</p>

<figure class="diagram" data-reveal>
    <div class="diagram__panels">
      <div class="panel panel--bad">
        <div class="panel__art">
          <img src="{{ 'dhd-receptor-bad.webp' | asset_url }}" width="900" height="900" loading="lazy" alt="Simplified illustration of a molecule activating a receptor">
        </div>
        <p class="panel__name"><span class="panel__degree">Full</span> receptor&nbsp;activation</p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel panel--good">
        <div class="panel__art">
          <img src="{{ 'dhd-receptor-good.webp' | asset_url }}" width="900" height="900" loading="lazy" alt="Simplified illustration of partial receptor activation">
        </div>
        <p class="panel__name"><span class="panel__degree">Partial</span> receptor&nbsp;activation</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </figure>

<p class="mechanism__coda">It didn’t explain everything about my Saturday.</p>
<p>But I wasn’t looking at a mystery bottle anymore.</p>
<p>I was looking at a plant with a named compound, and I kept reading.</p>

<p class="story-cta"><a class="btn btn--story" href="#claim-offer">See the capsules Dale sent me</a></p>

</div>
</section>

<article class="col body story reveal-name">

<h2 class="story-turn" data-reveal>Then I Saw the Other Name, and I Almost Threw the Jar Out</h2>

<p>A few pages in, I found out the plant had another name: <strong>kratom</strong>.</p>
<p>My stomach dropped. I pictured the pill bags by the gas station register. Maybe you did too.</p>
<p>But those pills aren’t the leaf.</p>
<p class="spine" data-reveal>They’re synthetic. Kratom leaf is&nbsp;not.</p>
<p>The natural leaf holds more than 40 compounds.</p>
<p>One of them is <strong>7-OH</strong>, a natural trace compound found at about <strong>1&nbsp;part in&nbsp;10,000</strong>.</p>

<figure class="fig" data-reveal><div class="fig__frame fig__frame--plain"><img src="{{ 'dhd-r25-rice.webp' | asset_url }}" alt="A bowl of about ten thousand white rice grains with a single red grain circled" width="1600" height="1200" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>1 grain in 10,000. About 100 parts per million.</figcaption></figure>

<p>Chemists make 7-OH in bulk, press it into pills, and sell them as kratom.</p>
<p>That trace in the leaf gives them a way to borrow the label.</p>

<aside class="pull pull--compare" data-reveal><p>Calling those pills kratom is like a jet wearing one feather and calling itself a&nbsp;bird.</p></aside>

<p>The DEA is in the process of banning concentrated 7-OH. The natural leaf isn’t the target.</p>

<figure class="fig fig--split" data-reveal><div class="fig__frame"><img src="{{ 'dhd-r24-split.webp' | asset_url }}" alt="Left, in colour: a fresh leaf beside a plain jar of capsules. Right, in grey: bags of pressed tablets and a shot bottle on a gas station counter" width="1600" height="1067" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption class="split-labels"><span>Natural kratom leaf extract</span><span class="split-labels__bad">Synthetic 7-OH sold as&nbsp;“kratom”</span></figcaption></figure>

<p>I wanted the leaf Dale told me about.</p>
<p>So I went back to the label on my jar.</p>

